## Market read: matched coffees were flat

As of **September 6**, the [seven-day retail price snapshot](https://api.purveyors.io/v1/price-index/stats) showed a **0.2% aggregate rise**, classified as quiet, across 835 listings and 31 suppliers. The **549 listings matched across periods were flat in aggregate**. The small headline rise is not evidence that the same coffees broadly became more expensive: changes in the mix of listings can move the aggregate while matched-lot pricing stays flat.

The separate [all-market signal summary](https://api.purveyors.io/v1/market/signals?summary=true), spanning retail and wholesale, counted 178 signals: 167 below-benchmark listings, 10 supplier-score-based outliers, and one qualifying price drop. These are screening counts, not 178 newly discounted coffees. A below-benchmark listing is not necessarily a bargain; bag size, crop age, quality, freight, and the comparison group still matter.

Coverage also changed: [edition 002](/blog/market-brief-002) captured 593 listings and 24 suppliers on September 1. More coverage can produce more signals without an improvement in buying conditions. This snapshot does not identify the price-drop lot or establish a like-for-like change in the number of opportunities. **Use it to guide a shortlist, not to time a bulk purchase.** The linked data endpoints are live; the figures above are the captured edition snapshot.

## Buying shortlist: full-sack terms

Two coffees worth investigating for different sample questions—not verified bargains. Supplier pages were checked September 6; availability and terms can change. Prices below are green-coffee list prices, not landed costs. Flavor notes are the suppliers’ descriptions, not our tasting results.

- **[Juan Puerta / Spumm](/catalog?coffee=10688), Yellow Rooster:** Colombian Castillo, listed as **honey / co-ferment**, at **$10.90/lb in 89.52-lb sacks**, held in Tampa. The [supplier page](https://yellowroostercoffee.com/coffees/juan-puerta-spumm) lists rose, hibiscus, star fruit, and nectarine and offers a sample request. A useful candidate for a process-focused sample flight, provided you first establish the co-ferment ingredients and method. Sample size, sample cost, freight, and small-bag availability are not established here.
- **[Hambela Buku Sayasa natural, lot 39070-2](/catalog?coffee=10522), Royal Coffee:** **$6.65/lb in 132.45-lb bags**, spot at Vancouver’s Seaforth warehouse. The [supplier page](https://royalcoffee.com/product/3427097000057688260/) identifies smallholder growers and **EDN Ethiopian Coffee, an independent processor and exporter**. Royal describes a 24-hour cold-water fermentation of cherry followed by three to four weeks of raised-bed drying, with blueberry, raspberry, cherimoya, watermelon, floral, and sweet cream notes. Consider it for a natural-process comparison; ask for a sample and harvest-year confirmation. For a US buyer, confirm cross-border logistics and delivered cost before comparing the sticker price with domestic offers.

**Home roasters:** both listings are full-sack/full-bag formats. Neither source establishes retail-size purchasing. A sample request is an inquiry, not a promise of a home-roasting quantity. The price difference between these two coffees does not establish relative value across different origins, processes, bag sizes, and warehouses.

## Traceability: ask for the evidence behind the label

A [Mongabay report republished by Daily Coffee News](https://dailycoffeenews.com/2026/09/03/ecuadors-deforestation-free-coffee-model-moves-beyond-the-pilot-stage/) describes Ecuador’s Deforestation-Free Coffee Initiative: 373 participating producers, farm-boundary mapping, forest-cover checks, and independent verification. It reports cumulative exports of 172.5 metric tons between 2022 and 2025—not that amount in 2025 alone.

For buyers evaluating a deforestation-free claim, the useful questions are specific: **Can the supplier connect this lot to mapped farms? What verification is available, and who maintains it?** The report also identifies technical assistance and scaling as unresolved challenges. A successful project is not proof that every Ecuadorian lot has equivalent documentation, nor a compliance determination for a particular shipment.

## Cafe operators: watch the leases, not an opening count

[7 Brew won an approximately $143.2 million bankruptcy auction](https://dailycoffeenews.com/2026/09/02/7-brew-wins-143-million-auction-for-former-salad-and-go-locations/) for assets including 73 former Salad and Go leases: 41 in Arizona, 20 in Texas, and six each in Nevada and Oklahoma. **The reported transaction remains subject to bankruptcy court approval.** These are potential sites, not 73 newly opened coffee shops.

For operators in those markets, court approval and actual site conversion are the next useful developments to watch. The auction alone does not establish an opening timetable, local sales impact, or increased demand for specialty green coffee.

## Off the cupping table

[Coffee People Zine is returning with Issue 26](https://dailycoffeenews.com/2026/09/04/coffee-people-zine-back-in-print-plans-minneapolis-party/): more than 150 pages from more than 80 contributors around the theme “enough.” DCN reports a public launch party on **September 20, 6:30–9:30 p.m., at FRGMNT Coffee inside Open Book in Minneapolis**. For readers interested in coffee people beyond purchasing, the new issue offers an offline look at the creative work of the coffee community.
